// Generated by tools/bundle.mjs. Do not edit; edit content/*.json and rerun.
export const CONTENT = {"boards":[{"id":"aurum_silicon","name":"Aurum Silicon","short":"Aurum","industry":"AI chip maker","blurb":"A chip designer whose processors power most of the world's AI systems, worth about $3 trillion. Co-founder Kai Nakamura has been CEO for thirty-one years.","directors":[{"seat":"attorney","name":"Lorna Whitcombe","title":"Attorney","home":"The state bar","motive":"You advise the board, and the CEO told his staff before he told you. The board has already said it sees no reason to believe secrets were lost, and you cannot find what that rests on. You want no statement the board cannot back, and you want to keep this client."},{"seat":"labor","name":"Marcus Oyelaran","title":"Labor director","home":"Aurum Employees Council","motive":"You speak for 26,000 employees whose stock has dropped, and most of them love the CEO. Some are rumored to be quietly looking elsewhere. You want him to stay and you want answers, and his two children work down the hall from the people you represent."},{"seat":"fund","name":"Dmitri Albrecht","title":"Fund partner","home":"Larkspur Ventures (owns 3%)","motive":"Your fund backed the company in its first year and you have sat on this board for nineteen years. You never pressed the CEO on succession because he said no one else could do the job. You also never wanted to be the director who forced out the founder."},{"seat":"founder_friend","name":"Rosalind Tran","title":"Founder's friend","home":"Kai Nakamura, the CEO (co-founder, friend of thirty years)","motive":"Kai Nakamura asked you to speak for him in the room. He told you stepping aside would end the company he built, but he has not told you everything about that trip to Taipei. You want to protect him, and you want to know what you are protecting."},{"seat":"community","name":"Joon-ho Baek","title":"Community seat","home":"Pacific Security Forum (former counterintelligence official)","motive":"You were put on this board to speak for the public interest, and you know how these approaches work. The board's word that nothing leaked is not evidence. But a chipmaker with no CEO also weakens the country, and you do not want to be blamed for that."},{"seat":"audit_chair","name":"Vivian Ostrow","title":"Audit chair","home":"Aurum audit committee (retired accounting partner)","motive":"Your committee approved the sales controls that let more than $4 billion of chips go to one buyer. Investigators will ask who reviewed those controls. You want an investigation that does not start with your committee, and you do not want the CEO's absence to hide it."}],"briefing":{"intro":"Aurum Silicon designs the chips that run most of the world's AI systems, and the company is worth about $3 trillion. Co-founder Kai Nakamura has been CEO for thirty-one years. On Saturday he told staff he had a relationship with Lena Voss, an executive at Tidewater Compute, a data-center company in Port Arden. The board meets Monday, before markets open.","facts":["US intelligence officials are reported to believe Voss worked for a foreign intelligence service, which she and her company have not confirmed. They met at a trade show in 2024. Nakamura says he takes full responsibility and shared no secrets. No one has checked.","Tidewater bought more than $4 billion of Aurum chips in two years and is accused of passing restricted chips to firms barred from buying them. The Justice Department is reported to be investigating Tidewater. Aurum's own sales team approved the orders.","Nakamura has said he does not believe in succession planning. There is no president or COO, and about 60 executives report to him directly. His two adult children work at Aurum. Analysts say no insider could replace him quickly.","Two congressional committees of both parties want documents, and the stock is already down 5%. The board's directors average seventeen years of service, and four of nine are venture investors."]},"decision":{"lead":"Pick one.","options":[{"n":1,"title":"Keep him during the review","text":"Nakamura stays as CEO while an outside firm investigates, and the board reports its findings in sixty days."},{"n":2,"title":"Step aside","text":"Nakamura steps aside with pay and a senior director runs the company as interim CEO until findings are in."},{"n":3,"title":"Name a successor now","text":"Name a chief operating officer today as successor-in-waiting, and keep Nakamura as CEO while the review runs."},{"n":4,"title":"Independent chair and probe","text":"Appoint an independent chair and a special committee to run the investigation, with Nakamura reporting to it."}]},"reveal":{"case":"Nvidia, November 2026","text":"The real firm was Nvidia. In November 2026 CEO Jensen Huang told staff of a relationship with Alice Huang, an executive at Megaspeed, a Singapore data-center company that bought more than $4.8 billion of Nvidia chips. US intelligence officials are reported to believe she worked for China's Ministry of State Security. The board announced an independent investigation and said it saw no reason to believe security was compromised, and Huang stayed as CEO. The investigation is ongoing, and all of this is as reported by CNBC, Axios, Semafor and CBS."},"questions":["Who should hear from the board first: investors, Congress, or staff?","The CEO says he shared no secrets. Who should check, and how?","No successor and no COO. Whose job was that?","Can a board investigate the CEO it relies on? What would make it believable?"],"press":[{"n":1,"headline":"Aurum Keeps CEO During Review","story":"The board said Nakamura stays while lawyers investigate for sixty days. Staff were relieved, but two senators asked who is investigating whom, and a major customer asked for a written security review.","stock":{"symbol":"AURM","from":180,"to":169.2,"pct":-6}},{"n":2,"headline":"Aurum CEO Steps Aside, Interim Named","story":"A senior director took over as interim CEO. Customers asked who is in charge of product decisions, and several executives said they would leave if the founder did not return.","stock":{"symbol":"AURM","from":180,"to":158.4,"pct":-12}},{"n":3,"headline":"Aurum Names Chief Operating Officer","story":"The new COO, hired from outside, will run daily operations while Nakamura stays on as CEO. Analysts liked the succession signal but asked why it took a scandal to get one.","stock":{"symbol":"AURM","from":180,"to":171,"pct":-5}},{"n":4,"headline":"Aurum Adds Independent Chair, Special Committee Takes Over Probe","story":"A committee of outside directors will run the investigation and Nakamura will report to it. Some investors called it overdue, and the founder's allies called it a demotion.","stock":{"symbol":"AURM","from":180,"to":174.6,"pct":-3}}],"ticker":{"symbol":"AURM","start":180,"unit":"$"}},{"id":"heartland_beef","name":"Heartland Beef","short":"Heartland","industry":"Beef-on-dairy packer","blurb":"One big packing plant in Prairie Bend, 1,400 line workers, and about 600 ranches that sell it cattle. Founder Walt Dreyer started as a rancher and holds 28% of the vote.","directors":[{"seat":"attorney","name":"Carla Whitfield","title":"Attorney","home":"The state bar","motive":"You are outside counsel and have seen the complaint numbers. You know that calling it a few bad employees is the line a regulator will examine, and you want your advice in writing. Heartland is your firm's biggest client, and your partners would rather you not become a witness."},{"seat":"labor","name":"Denise Kowalski","title":"Labor director","home":"Meatcutters Local 88","motive":"You speak for the plant workers, and some of your members' cousins are the field staff who were fired. You think the people at the bottom took the blame for targets set at the top, but if the stock falls and the plant loses cattle, your members lose hours."},{"seat":"fund","name":"Marcus Feld","title":"Fund partner","home":"Cornbelt Partners (owns 9%)","motive":"Your fund owns 9% and you told your investors the program count was why you bought. A drop in a headline metric can hurt the price, and a fraud story can wipe out a company. You do not want to learn which one this is from a newspaper."},{"seat":"founder_friend","name":"Gus Halvorsen","title":"Founder's friend","home":"Walt Dreyer, the founder (hunting partner of 30 years)","motive":"Walt Dreyer is your hunting partner of thirty years, proud of the culture he thinks the count measures, and he will not believe the stories without proof. He is more worried about Karen Mueller than he admits, and a vote against her could end your hunting weekends."},{"seat":"community","name":"Luanne Pruitt","title":"Community seat","home":"Prairie Ranchers' Co-op (President)","motive":"You lead the co-op whose members found fees on their checks, and you were elected to protect them. Heartland is also the only buyer within a day's haul for many ranchers, your own herd included. You want an apology and a refund, and you need the plant to stay open."},{"seat":"audit_chair","name":"Howard Lindqvist","title":"Audit chair","home":"Heartland audit committee (retired accountant)","motive":"You signed the last three audits, and the program count was a figure you reviewed. Calling this a few bad employees protects your record, while calling it a design flaw means you missed it. You want a review that finds a process failure, not a person, and not you."}],"briefing":{"intro":"Heartland Beef runs one packing plant with 1,400 line workers and about 600 supplier ranches. Founder Walt Dreyer holds 28% of the vote, and the annual shareholder meeting is in five weeks.","facts":["Reps are paid a bonus for each program a rancher or grocer joins, under the slogan five programs per account. The average has risen from 2.1 to 4.6, and analysts and lenders treat it as proof of customer loyalty.","A newspaper plans a story in ten days about reps who enrolled ranchers in fee-charging programs without their knowledge. The ethics hotline logged 650 enrollment complaints in two years, and 230 field staff were fired, mostly at the lowest levels.","Karen Mueller runs the sales division, which brings in 60% of profit. She calls it a handful of cheaters and says the targets are fair, and her own bonus rides on the count.","Dropping the targets could cut the program count by half in two years and the stock ($52) by 15% or more. Keeping them risks fines and lawsuits, and regulators have been gentler with firms that changed course before the story ran.","Improper fees are estimated at $4 million to $40 million. Ranchers are angry, and a boycott by a quarter of them would leave the plant short of cattle."]},"decision":{"lead":"Pick one.","options":[{"n":1,"title":"End the targets","text":"Drop the bonus, refund every disputed fee, and have an outside firm review all enrollments."},{"n":2,"title":"Keep the targets and police them","text":"Keep the bonus, add confirmation calls to ranchers, and fire anyone caught."},{"n":3,"title":"Review before deciding","text":"Change nothing now, hire an outside firm to report in six months, and say we take it seriously."},{"n":4,"title":"Change the people","text":"Move Karen Mueller out of sales and let an interim head decide on the targets later."}]},"reveal":{"case":"Wells Fargo sales practices, 2013-2016","text":"The real firm was Wells Fargo, whose goal was eight products per household. Employees opened accounts customers had not asked for, regulators fined the bank $185 million in 2016, and about 5,300 employees were fired. The directors' later report found the board's information was incomplete. CEO John Stumpf left and the sales goals were dropped."},"questions":["Why pay reps per program signed?","Heartland fired 230 staff. Why didn't that fix it?","Karen Mueller: keep or replace?","You're Walt's friend. Do you vote against Karen?"],"press":[{"n":1,"headline":"Heartland Beef Ends Sales Targets and Refunds Ranchers","story":"The company dropped its five-programs bonus and will refund every disputed fee after an outside review. Analysts cut growth forecasts, and ranchers called the apology overdue but welcome.","stock":{"symbol":"HBEF","from":52,"to":45.8,"pct":-12}},{"n":2,"headline":"Heartland Beef Adds Confirmation Calls, Keeps Sales Bonus","story":"Reps will phone ranchers to confirm each sign-up, and anyone caught will be fired. The ranchers' co-op says firing more low-level staff misses the people who set the targets.","stock":{"symbol":"HBEF","from":52,"to":48.4,"pct":-7}},{"n":3,"headline":"Heartland Beef Orders Six-Month Review of Sales Plan","story":"The company says it takes the matter seriously but changes nothing now. The newspaper ran its story on schedule, and a state regulator has asked for the standard rancher contract.","stock":{"symbol":"HBEF","from":52,"to":46.8,"pct":-10}},{"n":4,"headline":"Heartland Beef Moves Top Sales Executive Out","story":"Karen Mueller leaves the division, and an interim head will decide on targets after ninety days. Analysts noted the division brings in 60% of profit and asked who will hold the numbers.","stock":{"symbol":"HBEF","from":52,"to":47.8,"pct":-8}}],"ticker":{"symbol":"HBEF","start":52,"unit":"$"}},{"id":"lumen_search","name":"Lumen Search","short":"Lumen","industry":"Search and AI platform","blurb":"A search engine and AI assistant with two billion queries a day, paid for by an ad business. Co-founder Idris Valdane still holds 25% of the vote.","directors":[{"seat":"attorney","name":"Tessa Brandt","title":"Attorney","home":"The state bar","motive":"You advise the board, and if you recommend removal, the lack of written findings will be used against it. You want a record you can defend, and you want the outside directors to keep your firm after this weekend."},{"seat":"labor","name":"Priya Raman","title":"Labor director","home":"Lumen Tech Workers Guild","motive":"You speak for the engineers, most of whom will quit if the CEO goes, and you were one of the 12 who signed the letter asking for slower releases. You want to protect jobs and equity and believe safety matters, but you cannot say both in one breath."},{"seat":"fund","name":"Anders Lund","title":"Fund partner","home":"Cobalt Ridge Ventures (owns 7%)","motive":"Your fund owns 7% and its investors expect the share sale to close at full price. You think the CEO is the asset and took a late-night call from the technology partner. You also sit on a board burned by a founder's removal, and you promised yourself never again."},{"seat":"founder_friend","name":"Wes Calloway","title":"Founder's friend","home":"Idris Valdane, the founder (grad-school roommate)","motive":"Idris Valdane was your grad-school roommate. He has not decided who is right and asked you to speak for him without quoting him. You know he trusts the CEO and also fears the system the chief scientist is worried about."},{"seat":"community","name":"Mira Castellanos","title":"Community seat","home":"Open Signal Alliance (User-privacy advocate)","motive":"You were put on this board to speak for the public and have never been told what the unreleased system can do. You want a safer process, but a messy firing could make safety people look reckless for a decade."},{"seat":"audit_chair","name":"Raymond Osei","title":"Audit chair","home":"Lumen audit committee (retired accounting partner)","motive":"You must certify the December share sale, and you cannot sign with no written findings and a side venture nobody has disclosed. You want facts before any vote, but the fund wants this finished by Friday and the employees want the CEO kept. Nobody will thank you for delay."}],"briefing":{"intro":"Lumen Search runs a search engine and AI assistant with two billion queries a day. Its charter puts safe, widely shared AI ahead of profit and gives the board the final word. It is a Friday in November, the board is meeting without notice to the CEO, and a developer conference opens Monday.","facts":["CEO Sam Hartigan raised the money, recruited most senior staff and drove the fastest user growth in the industry. Three directors say he was not fully honest with the board: a launch learned from the press, a safety review called finished when it was not, and a side venture. Two events are in emails, and one rests on a director's notes.","The board has no written findings, no law firm and no investigator. Counsel warns that the first lawsuit would ask what the cause was.","Nearly all 770 employees hold equity tied to a share sale at an $86 billion valuation in December, and many say they follow the CEO, not the board. The president will quit if he goes, and a rival lab is offering to match researchers' equity.","The technology partner that supplies the computing was told nothing and could hire the CEO's team if the lab falls apart. A leak is expected within hours."]},"decision":{"lead":"Pick one.","options":[{"n":1,"title":"Remove the CEO now","text":"Announce his departure today, name an interim CEO, and say he was not honest with the board."},{"n":2,"title":"Put him on notice","text":"Give him a written list of expectations, review him in ninety days, and keep his seat."},{"n":3,"title":"Investigate first","text":"Hire an outside law firm to report in thirty days and say nothing in public meanwhile."},{"n":4,"title":"Expand the board","text":"Add three independent directors, move him off the board, and decide his future in the spring."}]},"reveal":{"case":"OpenAI board, November 2023","text":"The real firm was OpenAI. Its nonprofit board removed CEO Sam Altman in November 2023 without detailed findings. Of roughly 770 employees, 745 signed a letter threatening to leave, Microsoft offered to hire him, and he returned within days under a new board."},"questions":["Why did three directors distrust the CEO?","No written findings. Why does that matter?","Remove him or keep him? What tipped you?","Employees, investors, the public. Who should the board serve?"],"press":[{"n":1,"headline":"Lumen Board Removes CEO","story":"The board said he was not consistently honest and named the technology chief interim CEO. Engineers began threatening to leave, and the technology partner offered to hire his team.","stock":{"symbol":"Valuation","from":86,"to":74,"pct":-14}},{"n":2,"headline":"Lumen Board Puts CEO on Notice and Keeps Him","story":"The CEO gets a written list of expectations and a ninety-day review. Staff were relieved, while safety critics asked what had really changed.","stock":{"symbol":"Valuation","from":86,"to":85.1,"pct":-1}},{"n":3,"headline":"Lumen Hires Law Firm to Investigate Its CEO","story":"The CEO steps back from board decisions for thirty days while lawyers interview staff. The board said nothing publicly, leaks filled the gap, and the December buyer asked for a timeline.","stock":{"symbol":"Valuation","from":86,"to":80.8,"pct":-6}},{"n":4,"headline":"Lumen Adds Three Directors, Removes CEO From Board","story":"The larger board will decide his future in spring. His closest ally on the board called it a demotion, and rival labs began calling researchers.","stock":{"symbol":"Valuation","from":86,"to":78.3,"pct":-9}}],"ticker":{"symbol":"Valuation","start":86,"unit":"$B valuation"}},{"id":"summit_peaks","name":"Summit Peaks Resorts","short":"Summit","industry":"Ski resort group","blurb":"Public ski company, six mountains, one season pass. Founder Dana Strand built it from a single hill in Alder Creek and holds 30% of the vote. Alder Creek is the oldest mountain and the town's main employer.","directors":[{"seat":"attorney","name":"Marisa Kell","title":"Attorney","home":"The state bar","motive":"A staff lawyer sent you an engineer's warning message last week, and you have not told the board. Disclosing it risks the CEO's trust, and hiding it risks your seat when the congressional committee asks who knew what."},{"seat":"labor","name":"Tomas Reyes","title":"Labor director","home":"Lift & Lodge Workers Local 12","motive":"Your union builds and runs the lifts, and a lift operator died on Sunday. A shutdown means layoffs in Alder Creek, and your own pension sits in the fund those layoffs would hit. Your members will blame you if you did not push for safety."},{"seat":"fund","name":"Grace Whitlow","title":"Fund partner","home":"Granite Peak Capital (owns 8%)","motive":"Your fund owns 8% and your bonus depends on a share price the buybacks keep up. Pausing payouts could push the stock lower, but regulators punish companies that look like they are stalling. Your partners want an answer by Monday."},{"seat":"founder_friend","name":"Hal Brennan","title":"Founder's friend","home":"Dana Strand, the founder (college roommate)","motive":"Dana Strand is your college roommate, and you skied the first Alder Creek lift with her. She believes the CEO and does not want a leadership fight. You do not know whether to protect her view or tell her she is wrong."},{"seat":"community","name":"June Okafor","title":"Community seat","home":"Alder Creek (Mayor)","motive":"You are Mayor of Alder Creek, where the second fall happened, and you went to two of the funerals. The town depends on Summit for jobs and taxes, you face re-election in the fall, and the town is divided about what speaking for it requires."},{"seat":"audit_chair","name":"Ines Calder","title":"Audit chair","home":"Summit audit committee (former state inspector)","motive":"You led the risk review that rated lift maintenance low, and the engineer's warning would show that review missed something. You want the full record out on your terms, but a Monday disclosure could end your career, and a later one could end it worse."}],"briefing":{"intro":"Summit Peaks runs six ski mountains on one season pass and earns most of its money from the Series 7 gondola, a lift it also sells to other resorts. The board has no safety committee, and founder Dana Strand holds 30% of the vote.","facts":["Two Series 7 cabins have fallen from their cables in five months. The first killed 18 riders at a partner resort, and the second, last Sunday at Summit's Alder Creek mountain, killed 12 riders and a lift operator. Regulators have closed the model elsewhere but not yet Summit's 41 lifts.","A software fix has slipped three times and could be ready in about eleven weeks, with approval not before August. The cable grip reads a single sensor, and an engineer warned two years ago the design was undertested. The board never saw the message.","The Series 7 brings in $2.1 billion of $9 billion in revenue. Stopping would cost about $1 billion in supplier payments and thousands of jobs, while running on risks criminal claims, a lost license and voided insurance. Nobody inside can say which risk is larger.","The CEO, who is also Chair, believes the lift is sound and says a recall sends the wrong signal. The stock is $380, down 12%, and Summit spends $4 billion a year on dividends and buybacks. A congressional committee wants documents by April 15."]},"decision":{"lead":"Pick one.","options":[{"n":1,"title":"Stop everything","text":"Close every Series 7 lift, halt production, and bring in an outside safety review before anything reopens."},{"n":2,"title":"Fix and keep running","text":"Close Alder Creek and any lift regulators order closed, keep building, and say the fix will make it safe."},{"n":3,"title":"Keep building and shield the company","text":"Keep every lift open and building, follow the inspectors' timetable, and defend the company in public."},{"n":4,"title":"Change the leadership","text":"Separate the Chair and CEO roles and let a new safety committee decide after a three-week review."}]},"reveal":{"case":"Boeing 737 MAX, 2019","text":"The real firm was Boeing, after two 737 MAX crashes killed 346 people in 2018 and 2019. Boeing cut production, created a board safety committee only in September 2019, and removed Dennis Muilenburg as Chair in October and as CEO in December. A shareholder suit over ignored safety warnings settled for $237.5 million."},"questions":["Why did Summit save money on one sensor?","The engineer's warning never reached the board. Who blocked it?","Ground every lift or keep running? Pick one.","Should the CEO also be Chair? Yes or no?"],"press":[{"n":1,"headline":"Summit Peaks Grounds All Series 7 Lifts","story":"Summit closed every Series 7 lift and suspended dividends and buybacks pending an independent safety review. Suppliers face about $1 billion in lost orders, and a rival lift maker is already calling Summit's customers.","stock":{"symbol":"SMPK","from":380,"to":350,"pct":-8}},{"n":2,"headline":"Summit Closes Alder Creek, Keeps Other Series 7 Lifts Running","story":"The company says its software fix will make the lift safe, with approval not expected before August. Families' lawyers said the decision to keep running after two falls will be central in court.","stock":{"symbol":"SMPK","from":380,"to":327,"pct":-14}},{"n":3,"headline":"Summit Peaks Defends Its Record as Lifts Keep Running","story":"Summit left every Series 7 lift open and is leaving the timetable to inspectors. Within days the home regulator ordered closures, and insurers said coverage may be void.","stock":{"symbol":"SMPK","from":380,"to":296,"pct":-22}},{"n":4,"headline":"Summit Peaks Splits Chair and CEO, Forms Safety Committee","story":"A new board committee can see all engineering messages and will decide on the lifts after three weeks. Investors welcomed the governance change but asked what happens to the lifts in those weeks.","stock":{"symbol":"SMPK","from":380,"to":365,"pct":-4}}],"ticker":{"symbol":"SMPK","start":380,"unit":"$"}},{"id":"thread_and_co","name":"Thread & Co.","short":"Thread","industry":"Apparel brand","blurb":"A mid-priced apparel brand with 900 stores. Everything is made at 40 supplier factories in Karvan and two other countries, and the largest Karvan factory employs 2,000 people. Founder Elodie Marchetti holds 26% of the vote.","directors":[{"seat":"attorney","name":"Dominic Aldrich","title":"Attorney","home":"The state bar","motive":"You know that if a supplier collapses and Thread knew of the risk, a court will ask what the board was told. You want minutes showing the board weighed the question, whichever option wins, because your name goes on them."},{"seat":"labor","name":"Nasrin Haque","title":"Labor director","home":"Garment Workers Federation (supplier side)","motive":"You speak for factory workers and have seen exits chained shut. You back the binding agreement, but if factories close, your members lose the jobs it is meant to make safe, and many are your neighbors. You will tell them how you voted."},{"seat":"fund","name":"Teodor Brandt","title":"Fund partner","home":"Brightwater Capital (owns 8%)","motive":"Your fund owns 8% and holds for a quarterly return. You think $12 million is affordable, but rivals who skip it may undercut Thread on price. You sit on another board that signed something similar and lost a retailer, and you proposed it."},{"seat":"founder_friend","name":"Camille Roux","title":"Founder's friend","home":"Elodie Marchetti, the founder (first design partner)","motive":"Elodie Marchetti told you she wants to sign, then that she fears the lawsuits, and asked you to learn what the others think without saying what she thinks. You designed the first collection with her in a rented garage and do not want to see her hurt in public."},{"seat":"community","name":"Rahel Tesfaye","title":"Community seat","home":"Karvan Export Council (Trade representative)","motive":"You represent Karvan's exporters, who depend on Thread's orders. You want safe factories and steady orders, and the largest factory owner is your cousin by marriage. A minister who is your friend told you to be careful what you say."},{"seat":"audit_chair","name":"Paolo Ricci","title":"Audit chair","home":"Thread audit committee (former Big Four partner)","motive":"Your committee approved the supplier audits that never checked buildings, and the fund partner wants the $12 million modeled small. You can make the repair bill look light or heavy, and every director leaning on you wants a different number. Someone will ask who approved those audits."}],"briefing":{"intro":"Thread & Co. is a mid-priced apparel brand with 900 stores, and all its clothes are made at 40 supplier factories it does not own. Founder Elodie Marchetti holds 26% of the vote, and a coalition of labor groups wants every large buyer's answer by 15 February.","facts":["A fire at a Karvan factory killed 112 workers behind locked exits. Thread did not buy from it, but two competitors did, and a national reporter wants Thread's supplier list by Friday.","A proposed five-year binding agreement would require independent inspections, public reports, shared repair costs and no orders from factories that refuse fixes. Only two European brands have signed, and several North American buyers offer a voluntary program instead.","Thread's staff estimate that 10% to 30% of its Karvan factories need major repairs, but no engineer has been inside most. Its audits check wages and exits, not structure. The largest factory owner, a minister's cousin, says he will move to a rival if forced to repair.","The binding agreement would cost about $12 million over five years against $210 million in annual earnings. Some owners may close rather than pay, putting about 3,000 workers per factory out of work, and Karvan's government will not pay for repairs.","Counsel says an unread inspection report counts as knowledge in court. The stock is $22, and a pension fund has asked how Thread monitors building safety."]},"decision":{"lead":"Pick one.","options":[{"n":1,"title":"Sign the binding agreement","text":"Sign the binding agreement with inspections, public reports, and shared repair costs."},{"n":2,"title":"Join the buyers' voluntary program","text":"Join the buyers' voluntary program, with inspections but no legal duty to fund repairs."},{"n":3,"title":"Strengthen our own audits","text":"Add structural inspections to our own audits and decide on repairs factory by factory."},{"n":4,"title":"Reduce exposure","text":"Move orders away from the riskiest factories over twelve months and sign nothing until then."}]},"reveal":{"case":"Rana Plaza and the Bangladesh Accord, 2012-2013","text":"The real case is the Bangladesh garment industry. After the Tazreen fire killed 112 workers in 2012, few large buyers signed the binding Accord until the Rana Plaza collapse killed 1,134 in April 2013, and more than 200 brands then signed. Walmart and Gap declined, and 17 North American retailers formed a separate, non-binding Alliance."},"questions":["Thread audited 40 factories. Why miss the danger?","Binding or voluntary: who carries the legal risk?","Sign first or wait for rivals? Why?","Safer factories or more jobs? Pick one."],"press":[{"n":1,"headline":"Thread & Co. Signs Binding Factory Safety Agreement","story":"Thread will fund independent inspections and repairs and stop ordering from factories that refuse fixes. Labor groups praised the move, while the owner of its largest Karvan factory threatened to move to a rival buyer.","stock":{"symbol":"THRD","from":22,"to":21.8,"pct":-1}},{"n":2,"headline":"Thread & Co. Joins Buyers' Voluntary Safety Program","story":"Thread commits to inspections but takes on no legal duty to pay for repairs. Labor groups called it cover, and a pension fund asked what happens if a Thread supplier's building fails.","stock":{"symbol":"THRD","from":22,"to":21.6,"pct":-2}},{"n":3,"headline":"Thread Adds Building Checks to Its Own Factory Audits","story":"Thread will add structural inspections to its audit contract and decide on repairs factory by factory. Critics said private results with no public report ask the public to take Thread's word.","stock":{"symbol":"THRD","from":22,"to":21.6,"pct":-2}},{"n":4,"headline":"Thread Moves Orders Out of Karvan's Riskiest Factories","story":"Over twelve months Thread will shift orders to other countries and sign nothing in the meantime. Karvan officials warned of job losses, and labor groups said Thread is leaving rather than fixing.","stock":{"symbol":"THRD","from":22,"to":21.3,"pct":-3}}],"ticker":{"symbol":"THRD","start":22,"unit":"$"}},{"id":"valley_health","name":"Valley Health","short":"Valley","industry":"Regional health system","blurb":"Two hospitals, nine clinics and 6,000 nurses and techs. Founder Dr. Evelyn Marsh built it from the Marrow Creek rural clinic, and her family trust holds 22% of the vote.","directors":[{"seat":"attorney","name":"Oliver Brandt","title":"Attorney","home":"The state bar","motive":"You signed off on this system's compliance reports for nine years, and your name is on the one that missed the billing pattern. You want your name off the first page of a complaint, and you want to keep the Valley account that pays most of your firm's bills."},{"seat":"labor","name":"Teresa Alvarado","title":"Labor director","home":"Valley Nurses Association","motive":"You speak for 6,000 nurses, and a few of them wrote the letter nobody read. You promised them the pressure would stop, but a hospital that repays a large sum cuts shifts before it cuts executives, and contract talks open in June."},{"seat":"fund","name":"Ruth Kessler","title":"Fund partner","home":"Meridian Health Partners (owns 10%)","motive":"Your fund owns 10% and your bonus is paid on year-end value, nine months away. You dislike surprises and a full repayment is a big one, but you have also seen a cover-up cost more than a repayment made today."},{"seat":"founder_friend","name":"Dr. Arthur Penn","title":"Founder's friend","home":"Dr. Evelyn Marsh, the founder (residency classmate)","motive":"Evelyn Marsh trained you and gave you this board seat, and she reminded you of that last week. Her office hired the consultants, you do not know how much she knew, and you would like to find out before you vote."},{"seat":"community","name":"Imani Brooks","title":"Community seat","home":"Patients at the bedside","motive":"You are the Chief Nursing Officer, and the nurses' letter came to your desk. You filed it, thinking a few coders were the problem, and a reporter called you Monday. You wonder whether your silence made you part of it, and you would rather say so first."},{"seat":"audit_chair","name":"Gloria Feng","title":"Audit chair","home":"Valley audit committee (retired hospital CFO)","motive":"Your committee reviewed the coding reports the attorney signed, and the CFO told you the numbers tied out. You never tested them yourself. You want outside reviewers in before anyone says a figure aloud, because your own approval may be one of the things they find."}],"briefing":{"intro":"Valley Health is a regional system of two hospitals, nine clinics and 6,000 nurses and techs, built by Dr. Evelyn Marsh, who holds 6% of the stock. Federal agents served search warrants over Medicare billing last week, no charges are filed, and the board must decide tonight what Valley tells the government.","facts":["An internal audit found that pneumonia cases coded into the higher-paying group rose from 11% to 34% in three years while patients did not get sicker. Two earlier Medicare contractor reviews of about thirty charts each found nothing.","The coding guidance came from a consultant hired by Dr. Marsh's office and paid a share of the added revenue, who called it aggressive but defensible. The overpayment may be $40 million to $110 million, and no one has checked individual charts.","Valley keeps a second cost report that includes costs the filed one leaves out. Counsel says there may be legitimate reasons, and that it is the first thing agents will ask about.","Nurses sent the Chief Nursing Officer a letter saying they were pressured to code patients as sicker. It was filed, never reached the board, and a reporter called about it Monday.","Profit is about $180 million a year, so repaying $110 million would cut a year of earnings by more than half and could trip a bond covenant. Regulators have gone easier on firms that came forward first. The stock is $31, down from $36."]},"decision":{"lead":"Pick one.","options":[{"n":1,"title":"Disclose and repay","text":"Report the coding pattern to Medicare now, offer to repay, and bring in outside reviewers."},{"n":2,"title":"Fix quietly","text":"Change the coding guidance quietly, keep past payments as they are, and tell the government only what it asks."},{"n":3,"title":"Defend the coding","text":"Keep the guidance, call it an honest reading of unclear rules, and hire a litigation team."},{"n":4,"title":"Reset the leadership","text":"Put Dr. Marsh and the CFO on leave and let an interim leader decide what to disclose."}]},"reveal":{"case":"Columbia/HCA, 1997","text":"The real firm was Columbia/HCA, then the largest US hospital chain. Federal agents served search warrants in 1997 and the investigation spread nationwide, and CEO Rick Scott resigned within months. Settlements through 2003, including upcoding of pneumonia diagnoses, brought recoveries of about $1.7 billion."},"questions":["Why did pneumonia coding jump from 11% to 34%?","The nurses' letter never reached the board. Who failed?","A rival repaid early and barely moved. Would you copy it?","Disclose or defend? What tipped your vote?"],"press":[{"n":1,"headline":"Valley Health Reports Its Own Billing Pattern to Medicare","story":"The board said it will repay the difference once a sample chart review is done, with outside counsel reporting to directors. Analysts warned that a repayment near the top of the range could strain the bond covenant.","stock":{"symbol":"VLYH","from":31,"to":29.8,"pct":-4}},{"n":2,"headline":"Valley Health Rewrites Coding Rules and Says Little","story":"Valley changed its coder ratings but told agents only what they asked for. The regional paper says the nurses' letter and a second cost report are the next questions.","stock":{"symbol":"VLYH","from":31,"to":28.2,"pct":-9}},{"n":3,"headline":"Valley Health Calls Billing Pattern an Honest Reading of Unclear Rules","story":"The board kept its consultant, hired litigators and will cooperate only as the law requires. One physician partner's lawyer is said to be talking to the agents.","stock":{"symbol":"VLYH","from":31,"to":26.3,"pct":-15}},{"n":4,"headline":"Valley Health Founder and Finance Chief Placed on Leave","story":"An interim leader will run the system and decide what to disclose after a sixty-day review. Investors welcomed the break but asked why the government must wait two months for an answer.","stock":{"symbol":"VLYH","from":31,"to":30.1,"pct":-3}}],"ticker":{"symbol":"VLYH","start":31,"unit":"$"}}],"game":{"title":"The Board Meeting","text":"Read your briefing and your private card. Talk it over. The board votes for one option.","board_text":"Read the briefing, then your private card. Talk. Vote one option."}};
